// buffer_reuse — PMT Wave 8a: sequential states reuse buffer design
// Expected exit code: 30
//
// Allocates state p, "consumes" it by passing it to a function that
// reads p.x, then allocates state q. Under Wave 8a's "sum of all state
// sizes" buffer sizing, p and q get DIFFERENT offsets (p at 0, q at 16)
// — there is no live-range-based reuse yet. The test verifies that the
// single-buffer design correctly handles sequential state lifetimes:
// q's slot is fresh (not aliased to p's), so writing to q does not
// affect the value already read from p.
//
// This is a forward-looking test for the future live-range-based buffer
// reuse optimisation: when state p's live range ends (after consume(p)
// returns), a future wave could assign q the SAME offset as p (slot 0)
// instead of a fresh slot (16). The "sum" sizing would then shrink from
// 32 bytes to 16 bytes. This test passes either way (the observable
// behaviour is identical); it documents the intended reuse pattern.
//
// VUMA Key Concepts:
//   - state lifetime: p is "consumed" (read) before q is allocated
//   - the single buffer is sized for the SUM of state sizes (no reuse yet)
//   - consume(p) reads p.x; subsequent q.x = 20 must not corrupt the
//     already-read value of p.x
//   - future live-range analysis would let q reuse p's slot

layout Cell = { v: u32 }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let c = state_new(Cell);
    c.v = 30;
    return c.v;
}
